{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ומראה", :translit "u-mar'eh", :gloss "and the appearance"} {:he "תגין על הה\"א", :translit "tagin al ha-hei", :gloss "tags on the heh"} {:he "נוגה", :translit "nogah", :gloss "radiance"} {:he "ובה' אור", :translit "uv-ha- or", :gloss "and through the heh, light"} {:he "אור", :translit "or", :gloss "light"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Tagin (crownlets) drawn on the letter heh of ומראה signal that God's appearance shines through the letter heh itself, the same letter prefixed to words for radiance and light."}], :frame {:text "The letters of the divine name serve as conduits of divine light: Proverbs 30:4 asks 'what is his name and what is his son's name,' which the tradition reads as pointing to the letters of God's name as channels of revelation (Menahot 29b). The letter heh specifically represents divine radiance, as the breath of its pronunciation requires no articulation, symbolizing effortless divine emanation (Menahot 29b). Ezekiel's vision describes divine radiance using the term נוגה (Ezekiel 1:4, 1:27, 8:2), and Genesis 1:3 records 'Let there be light (אור),' the first act of divine self-disclosure.", :sources ["Proverbs 30:4" "Menahot 29b" "Ezekiel 1:4" "Ezekiel 1:27" "Ezekiel 8:2" "Genesis 1:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Tagin (crownlets) drawn on the letter heh of ומראה signal that God's appearance shines through the letter heh itself, the same letter prefixed to words for radiance and light.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:28:37.828227022Z", :model "glm-5.2"}